      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**Scope** — panels are scoped to **all switches in the SU(s) the job occupies** (`$job_switches`), because a collective traverses the whole SU CLOS (leaf **and** spine tiers). The bottom table is the job's *exact* rails via the `node_ib_link` bridge (node→leaf:port).\n\n**Reliable signal = the degraded-port gauge** (`ib:node:port_slow==1`, red tiles). Error-counter **totals are deliberately NOT shown**: `increase()` over a long range inflates across an exporter/UFM counter reset (the retracted '5.9M symbol errors' was exactly this artifact). Link-down and discard panels use **`rate()` / peak-rate**, which correct for resets per-interval.\n\n**Resolution** — fabric counters are **minute-cadence** (UFM ~78 s) → fabric *health/cause*, not per-second step jitter. GPU **power** (top panel) is the 2nd-resolution training-progress proxy (NCCL comm-stalls dip power while util stays ~100%). *True per-collective step time* is not yet collected — recommend piloting the **NCCL Inspector profiler exporter** (`nccl_collective_exec_time_microseconds{slurm_job_id,node}`, 30 s cadence) for the primary slowness axis.\n\n**Reading it — the regression test:** **job 417** ran SLOW (66 s/step, worst jitter) yet its fabric was **CLEAN** (zero link-down/symbol/discard on its SU1 rails). *Error counters ≠ steady slowness.* If this board renders 417 as fabric-error-heavy, it has re-created the `increase()` artifact and is WRONG. A genuinely fabric-hurt run shows power dips aligned with link-down/discard **rate** bursts + degraded ports on *its* path. Flat fabric + slow job ⇒ look elsewhere (data pipeline, checkpoint I/O, a straggler GPU). Other SUs' errors are **not** shown, by design."
